WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.775
Idea. Your school locks the staff room, gives you your own login, and lets only teachers change your marks. A computer network is protected in the same three ways.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:19.679
Networks need protecting because malware can spread between computers, and outsiders can try to get in and steal or change data.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:33.571
Authentication checks that a user really is who they claim to be. Methods include a username and password, a security question, biometrics such as a fingerprint, and two-factor authentication.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:42.873
Physical security protects the hardware itself: locked server rooms, swipe cards, C C T V cameras and security guards.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:55.883
Access rights give each user only what their job needs. The rights are none, read, write and execute. A teacher can change the marks file, but a student can only read their own.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:09.697
Do not mix up validation and authentication. Validation checks that data is sensible, like a password being long enough. Authentication checks identity, like the password matching the stored one.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:27.471
Method. Decide what the threat is. Pick a control that matches it: authentication for identity, physical security for hardware, access rights for data. Never reuse a method the question already gives. To describe, name it, then say how it works.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:44.022
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper 1 in June 2024. Employees already log in with a biometric. Describe one other measure to make sure an employee is who they claim to be. It is worth two marks.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:52.173
Biometrics are already used, so cross them out. Another way to prove identity is two-factor authentication.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:02:08.994
Describe it. After their password, the employee also types a one-time code sent to their phone. Only the real employee should have both, so it confirms who they are. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:19.465
Common mistake. A common mistake is writing password on its own. Say that each user has a unique password, checked against a stored record.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:31.759
Another mistake is giving encryption or backups as ways to stop people getting in. They protect data after access. Entry is controlled by authentication and firewalls.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:48.419
Your turn. Now you try. A clinic server holds patient records. A cleaner, a nurse and a doctor work in the building. Suggest one physical measure for the server, and the access rights each person needs. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:03:01.114
Answer. Keep the server in a locked room with swipe card entry. The cleaner needs no access to the records. The nurse may need to read them. The doctor needs to read and write them.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:09.783
Remember the method: name the threat, match the control, then say how it works. Now try the practice questions for this type.
